## Remember `UserEmail`? 🤓

```rust
use validator::ValidateEmail;

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct UserEmail(String);

impl UserEmail {               +-------------------------+
    pub fn parse(s: String) -> |Result<UserEmail, String>| {
                               +-------------------------+
        if s.validate_email() {
           +-----------+
           |Ok(Self(s))|
           +-----------+
        } else {
           +-----------------------------------------------+
           |Err(format!("{} is not a valid user email", s))|
           +-----------------------------------------------+
        }
    }
}
```


## And since Result is an enum, we can `match` it 🤩

```rust
// validate_credentials:
//
//   Credentials -> Result<Uuid, LoginError>
//
match validate_credentials(credentials).await {
}                       +Choose action -----+
                        |1. Fill match arms |
                        +-------------------+
```


## Just fill in the blanks, or slap a `todo!` on it 🤷

```rust
// validate_credentials:
//
//   Credentials -> Result<Uuid, LoginError>
//
match validate_credentials(credentials).await {

    Ok(_) => todo!("handle the case where everything is OK"),

    Err(_) => todo!("add error handling"),

}
```


## Finally, we add the real code:

```rust
match validate_credentials(credentials).await {
  Ok(user_id) => {
    return HttpResponse::SeeOther()
      .insert_header((LOCATION, "/admin/dashboard"))
      .finish());
  }
  Err(e) => {
    return login_redirect(LoginError::AuthError(e));
  }
}
```

## "Do we have to `match` every Result?" 🤔


## No, we can use `.unwrap()`, among other things:

```rust
// Here, we're convinced we'll
// always have a port available
let port = listener.local_addr().unwrap().port();
```


## So why put ourselves through `match`? ☝🤓

!image(assets/unwrap-me-i-double-dare-you.jpg,,800)


## ...even the big players get caught out 💥

!image(assets/cloudflare-incident.png,https://blog.cloudflare.com/18-november-2025-outage/,1200,,,true)


## Save `.unwrap()` for tests, and learn to propagate or handle `Result` properly

```rust
fn my_function() -> Result<PortNumber, Error> {
  /* In this case, we SHOULD have an IP address. */
  /* If not, bubble up the error upwards */
  let port = listener.local_addr()?.port();
  return port;
}
```

