# Traits: turbocharged OOP

!image(assets/where-we-re-going-we-dont-need-classes.png,,1300)

/*
 Pas d'héritage, pas de "abstract static final etc.". Une composition simple de méthodes
*/


## We define what a `PaymentProcessor` is

```rust
pub struct RecipientEmail(pub String);
pub struct SenderEmail(pub String);

pub trait PaymentProcessor {
    fn send_payment(
        recipient: &RecipientEmail,
        sender: &SenderEmail,
    ) -> Result<(), anyhow::Error>;
}
```

## Then we implement "our own" `PaymentProcessor`

```rust
pub struct RecipientEmail(pub String);
pub struct SenderEmail(pub String);

pub trait PaymentProcessor {
    fn send_payment(
        recipient: &RecipientEmail,
        sender: &SenderEmail,
    ) -> Result<(), anyhow::Error>;
}

pub struct MyPaymentProcessor {}

impl PaymentProcessor for MyPaymentProcessor {}
```

## .[full-image]

```rust
pub struct RecipientEmail(pub String);
pub struct SenderEmail(pub String);

pub trait PaymentProcessor {
    fn send_payment(
        recipient: &RecipientEmail,
        sender: &SenderEmail,
    ) -> Result<(), anyhow::Error>;
}

pub struct MyPaymentProcessor {}

impl PaymentProcessor for MyPaymentProcessor {}
   +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
   |not all trait items implemented, missing: `send_payment`                      |
   |missing `send_payment` in implementation                                      |
   |                                                                              |
   |Related information:                                                          |
   |                                                                              |
   |  * main.rs#422,5: `send_payment` from trait                                  |
   |  * main.rs#431,47: implement the missing item: `fn send_payment(_:           |
   |&RecipientEmail, _: &SenderEmail) -> std::result::Result<(), anyhow::Error> { |
   |todo!() }`: `fn send_payment(_: &RecipientEmail, _: &SenderEmail) ->          |
   |std::result::Result<(), anyhow::Error> { todo!() }`                           |
   +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```


## Just like with enums, Rust's powerful language server can help

```rust
pub struct MyPaymentProcessor {}

impl PaymentProcessor for MyPaymentProcessor {}
      +Choose action --------------+
      |1. Implement missing members|
      +----------------------------+
```



## Then we add whatever implementation we want:

```rust
pub struct RecipientEmail(pub String);
pub struct SenderEmail(pub String);

pub trait PaymentProcessor {
    fn send_payment(
        recipient: &RecipientEmail,
        sender: &SenderEmail,
    ) -> Result<(), anyhow::Error>;
}

pub struct MyPaymentProcessor {}
impl PaymentProcessor for MyPaymentProcessor {
    fn send_payment(
        recipient: &RecipientEmail,
        sender: &SenderEmail,
    ) -> Result<(), anyhow::Error> {
        todo!("Payment instructions from {sender} to {recipient} go here !")
    }
}
```


## The "standard" traits
#### A simple example: I have a `LoginError` and I want to debug it

```rust
pub enum LoginError {
    AuthenticationError(String),
    UnexpectedError(String),
}
```

/*
 Par exemple, j'ai un test qui plante et je veux comprendre pourquoi
*/


## A super handy "function": `dbg!`

```rust
let my_error = LoginError::AuthError("Uh-oh!");
dbg!(&my_error)
```

## Except LoginError isn't a primitive type...

```rust
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|`LoginError` doesn't implement `std::fmt::Debug`         |
|add `#[derive(Debug)]` to `LoginError` or manually `impl |
|std::fmt::Debug for LoginError`                          |
|                                                         |
|Related information:                                     |
|                                                         |
|  * error.rs#53,18: required by a bound in `Error`       |
|  * post.rs#18,1: consider annotating `LoginError` with  |
|    `#[derive(Debug)]`                                   |
|                                                         |
| (rustc E0277)                                           |
|---------------------------------------------------------|
|https://doc.rust-lang.org/error-index.html#E0277         |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
```

/*
 Regardez la puissance du message d'erreur !
*/


## Deriving: simple and handy in 90% of cases

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum LoginError {
    AuthError(Error),
    UnexpectedError(Error),
}
```

## Fine for simple cases, but how do we implement `Debug` ourselves?

## Once again, the language server is here to help 😍

```rust
pub enum LoginError {
    AuthError(String),
    UnexpectedError(String),
}

impl std::fmt::Debug for LoginError {}
              +Choose action --------------+
              |1. Implement missing members|
              +----------------------------+
```

## We get a "default" implementation we can customize 🤤

```rust
impl std::fmt::Debug for LoginError {
  fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
    match self {
      Self::AuthError(arg0) =>
          f.debug_tuple("AuthError").field(arg0).finish(),
      Self::UnexpectedError(arg0) =>
          f.debug_tuple("UnexpectedError").field(arg0).finish(),
    }
  }
}
```

## We can put anything in it (as long as we follow the trait)

```rust
impl std::fmt::Debug for LoginError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut Formatter<'_>) -> Result {
        writeln!(f, "{}\n", e)?;
        let mut current = e.source();
        while let Some(cause) = current {
            writeln!(f, "Caused by:\n\t{}", cause)?;
            current = cause.source();
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

/*
 Ici j'utilise .source() pour remonter la chaîne d'erreurs
*/


## Now we get much clearer error details when debugging:

```log
Failed to log in user.
Caused by:
    Failed to retrieve user informations
Caused by:
    error returned from database
Caused by:
    table "user" does not exist
```


## A "blanket implementation" of one trait for all COMPATIBLE types

!image(assets/cozy-pepe.png,,800)

/*
 L'art d'automatiquement implémenter des méthodes pour d'autres types.
*/


## Idea: I want to customize how my variables are logged 🤔

```rust
trait Loggable { fn log(&self); }

pub struct Cat { pub name: String, }

fn main() {
    let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3];
    numbers.log(); // Error! 💥
    // Oh, right! I need to implement Loggable for u64 😫

    let s = String::from("Comfy blanket");
    s.log(); // Error! 💥
    // Oh, right! I need to implement Loggable for String 😫

    let c = Cat { name: "Mimouchka".to_string(), };
    c.log(); // Error! 💥
    // Oh, right! I need to implement Loggable for Cat 😫
}
```

/*
 Ça serait pas mal d'avoir à manuellement impl à chaque fois
*/


## `impl<T>`, because we're worth it 💇

```rust
trait Loggable { fn log(&self); }

// Anything that implements Debug now implements Loggable automatically! 🤩
impl<T> Loggable for T
where T: Debug {
    fn log(&self) { println!("[log] {:?}", self); }
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Cat { pub name: String, }

fn main() {
    let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3];
    numbers.log(); // Vec<i32> implements Debug out of the box, so Loggable too! 😌

    let s = String::from("Comfy blanket");
    s.log(); // String implements Debug out of the box, so Loggable too! 😌

    let c = Cat { name: "Astra".to_string(), };
    c.log(); // Cat implements Debug (via derive), so Loggable too! 😌
}
```


## And Rust has TONS of types, traits and impls like these 🤩

```
- `Option` (`Some<…>` or `None`)
- `Debug` vs `Display`
- `Deref`
- `Drop`
- ...and many more (plus all the community ones 💓)
```


